{"id":"u-250c54ac-c08c-4de4-8a56-96dd9e6feb77","src":"community","title":"MAD DOG","author":"Rob C.","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/E954dk-mad-dog","desc":"MAD DOG is a three-day powerbuilding program designed to maximize strength and muscular hypertrophy through heavy compound lifts, targeted accessory work, and progressive overload.\n\nCombining powerlifting and bodybuilding principles, MAD DOG emphasizes measurable strength gains, muscular development, and effective fatigue management. The philosophy is simple: train hard, recover effectively, and progress consistently.\n\nProgram Structure\n\nThree training sessions are performed sequentially, regardless of the days of the week.\n\nDay 1 — Hypertrophy: Moderate-to-higher repetitions targeting muscular size, balanced development, and controlled execution.\n\nDay 2 — Squat & Bench Strength: Heavy compound movements emphasizing lower-body and pressing strength.\n\nDay 3 — Deadlift & Overhead Press Strength: Heavy compound movements targeting posterior-chain, shoulder, and upper-back strength.\n\nRest days are incorporated as needed to optimize recovery and performance.\n\nDouble Progression\n\nMAD DOG uses double progression to systematically increase repetitions and resistance while respecting prescribed RPE targets.\n\nSelect a weight that allows the lower end of the prescribed repetition range at the target RPE.\n\nProgressively increase repetitions while maintaining proper technique and intensity.\n\nWhen any single working set reaches the upper repetition limit at the prescribed RPE, increase the weight at the next session.\n\nWith the heavier weight, work back toward the upper repetition limit and repeat.\n\nExample: For 3 × 3–5 @ RPE 7–8, reaching 5 repetitions on just one working set at the target RPE triggers a weight increase next session.\n\nIntensity Management\n\nTraining intensity is regulated using Rate of Perceived Exertion (RPE):\n\nRPE 7–8: Heavy compound lifts, approximately 2–3 reps in reserve.\n\nRPE 8–9: Hypertrophy movements, approximately 1–2 reps in reserve.\n\nRPE 9: Isolation movements, approximately 1 rep in reserve.\n\nThe goal is to maximize productive training while minimizing unnecessary fatigue.\n\nDeload Protocol\n\nDeloads are taken as needed, rather than on a fixed schedule, when accumulated fatigue begins affecting recovery or performance.\n\nReduce all working weights by 30% from the previous session.\n\nMaintain the same exercises, sets, and repetitions.\n\nAllow RPE to decrease naturally.\n\nResume normal loading once adequately recovered.\n\nExample: 200 lbs × 3 × 5 becomes 140 lbs × 3 × 5.\n\nThis approach reduces training stress while preserving movement practice and consistency. Additional recovery adjustments may be appropriate when necessary.\n\nTraining Philosophy\n\nMAD DOG prioritizes sustainable progression over unnecessary exhaustion. Heavy lifting builds strength, accessory work develops muscle, and double progression ensures consistent improvement.\n\nThe objective is to become stronger, build more muscle, and maintain long-term training consistency.\n\nTrain with intensity. Progress with purpose. Recover with discipline.","level":["Intermediate","Novice","Beginner","Advanced"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":60,"joins":1,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-10-25T15:31:12.797Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Day 1","Day 2","Day 3"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Incline Bench Press (Barbell)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8,"rpeHi":9,"x":2}]},{"n":"Pull-Up (Assisted)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8,"rpeHi":9,"x":2}]},{"n":"Hack Squat","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":12,"rpe":8,"rpeHi":9,"x":2}]},{"n":"Hyperextension","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":8,"rpeHi":9,"x":2}]},{"n":"Lateral Raise (Cable)","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":20,"rpe":9,"x":3}]},{"n":"Preacher Curl (EZ Bar)","sets":[{"lo":8,"hi":15,"rpe":9,"x":3}]},{"n":"Skull Crusher (EZ Bar)","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9,"x":3}]}]},{"ex":[{